Essentials commands to remember!  
 
A bit of context before starting Linux:  
Before understanding commands in Linux, you need to understand what a terminal is – which is essentially a command line interface that interacts with the OS (Operating System) which you can navigate your file system, install & configure software and many more.  
 
Command on its own cannot be understood by the OS – so instead it is interpreted by shell (will be covered later).  
 
What are (Linux) commands?  
Textual instruction that tells the OS what to do  
Have various options & arguments that can modify their behaviour  
Case sensitive ( ls & Ls could be two different things)  
Every command has their own instruction manual ( with the "man" command e.g. man ls – shows all the information you need about ls)  
 
Example : $ commands options arguments  
                         ls – a . Desktop  
 
Command 1: ls  
Lists the contents of the current directory (think of it as a folder) you are in  
Example : Desktop Documents Downloads  
Command 2: pwd  
Prints the current working directory  
Example: /home/abidkhan  
Command 3: cd  
Essentially changes the directory from your working one  
Example: You are in /home/abidkhan and have desktop in your current directory, command line "cd Desktop" will change it and "ls" will list what you have in your desktop  
(extension) cd .. - means changing directory to the previous one e.g. /home to / which is root directory  
Command 4: mkdir  
Makes a new directory  
Example: mkdir games – a folder called games in your desktop  
Command 5: rmdir  
Remove a empty current directory  
e.g. rmdir games  
Command 6: touch  
To create a file in the directory  
e.g. touch hello.txt  
Command 7: sudo  
Bypasses the permission to access the files which requests personal details from the user  
Extra info:  
Hidden directories start with a  "."  
To make a hidden directory e.g. mkdir .linux , if permission denied then add the sudo command and to check if it was made use the command along with the option "ls - a"